  "aiDetectorTriggerNoWatermark": "No Claude watermark",
  "aiDetectorWatermarkExplanation": "The text carries {count, plural, =1{one invisible character} other{{count} invisible characters}} that take up no space on screen but travel with the text through every copy and paste. Typing cannot produce them. luma reads that hidden payload as Claude’s watermark, which is why the verdict names Claude instead of hedging at \"AI-generated\".",
  "@aiDetectorWatermarkExplanation": {
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "aiDetectorSignatureExplanation": "The text identifies its own author — it names Claude or Anthropic in a product context, not as an ordinary person’s name. That is a direct attribution, so it outranks every style statistic below it.",
  "aiDetectorAssistantExplanation": "Stock chat-assistant phrasing shows the text came out of a conversation with a model. It does not say which model, so the verdict stays unnamed — this only raises the score.",
  "aiDetectorNoWatermarkExplanation": "No invisible watermark characters and no self-reference to Claude. That is not a clean bill of health: plenty of generated text carries no signature at all, so the style checks below still do the work.",
  "aiDetectorBurstinessExplanationHigh": "Every sentence hovers around the same length. Human writing usually swings between short punches and long rambles; steady lengths suggest one generator keeping a consistent rhythm.",
  "aiDetectorBurstinessExplanationLow": "Sentence lengths vary naturally, which human writers do and models find hard to fake.",
  "aiDetectorPhraseExplanationHigh": "These words and constructions appear far more often in model-generated prose than in everyday human writing. Each chip below is a literal match in your text.",
  "aiDetectorPhraseExplanationLow": "None of the stock phrases models lean on were found. That is not a point in the text’s favour — plenty of generated writing avoids them — so this check abstains rather than voting the score down.",
  "aiDetectorExtremesExplanationHigh": "Not one sentence is genuinely short and not one genuinely runs on. People write both without thinking about it; a generator settles into a safe middle band and stays there.",
  "aiDetectorExtremesExplanationLow": "The text contains both clipped sentences and long ones, the way unedited human writing usually does.",
  "aiDetectorVoiceExplanationHigh": "Nobody is on the page — barely an \"I\", \"we\" or \"you\", no casual wording, no direct questions. Generated prose defaults to this detached register. Formal human writing does too, which is why this counts for less than the rhythm checks.",
  "aiDetectorVoiceExplanationLow": "The text speaks in a personal voice, with pronouns and casual wording that generated prose rarely reaches for unprompted.",
  "aiDetectorFormattingExplanationHigh": "The text is laid out the way a chat model answers: headings, bullet runs and bolded labels. That structure travels with the text when it is pasted somewhere else.",
  "aiDetectorFormattingExplanationLow": "The text reads as prose rather than a formatted chat answer. Plenty of generated text is prose too, so this only counts when it fires.",
  "aiDetectorContrastExplanationHigh": "Sentences that set up a wrong answer only to knock it down — \"not just X, but Y\", \"the real question is\" — are the rhetorical move current models use to sound insightful.",
  "aiDetectorContrastExplanationLow": "None of the set-up-and-knock-down constructions models favour were found. Their absence means nothing on its own.",
  "aiDetectorOpenersExplanationHigh": "{used} of {total} sentences start with a formal connective. Essay-bot structure opens sections with \"Moreover,\" and \"Furthermore,\" far more than people do.",
  "@aiDetectorOpenersExplanationHigh": {
    "placeholders": {
      "used": {
        "type": "int"
      },
      "total": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "aiDetectorOpenersExplanationLow": "Sentences do not lean on formal connectives to start. That on its own says nothing about who wrote them.",
  "aiDetectorContractionsExplanationHigh": "Almost no contracted forms (\"don’t\", \"it’s\"). Generated text defaults to the stiffer un-contracted register.",
  "aiDetectorContractionsExplanationLow": "Contractions appear at a natural human rate.",
  "aiDetectorDashExplanationHigh": "Em dashes pepper the text well past what typical human prose uses — a much-memed model habit.",
  "aiDetectorDashExplanationLow": "Em-dash use is within normal range.",
  "aiDetectorDashExplanationNone": "No em dashes present, which proves nothing either way.",
  "aiDetectorPassiveExplanationHigh": "Heavy passive construction (\"was designed to\", \"is considered\") keeps agency out of sentences — common in generated prose.",
  "aiDetectorPassiveExplanationLow": "Passive voice stays at a normal level, which is true of most generated text as well.",
  "aiDetectorParagraphExplanationHigh": "All paragraphs are nearly the same size, like content poured evenly into containers. Human drafts lurch between short and long.",
  "aiDetectorParagraphExplanationLow": "Paragraph sizes differ naturally.",
  "aiDetectorRepeatedOpenersExplanationHigh": "\"{word}\" opens {count} sentences. Real writers rarely hammer one opener; template-driven generation does.",
  "@aiDetectorRepeatedOpenersExplanationHigh": {
    "placeholders": {
      "word": {
        "type": "String"
      },
      "count": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "aiDetectorRepeatedOpenersExplanationLow": "Sentence openers are varied, as they are in most writing of either origin.",
